The change modifies the condition from `len(a) < (i+1)` to `len(a) == 0`.

Looking at the Go `bufio.Reader.Peek` documentation: "Peek returns the next n bytes without advancing the reader. The bytes stop being valid at the next read call. If Peek returns fewer than n bytes, it also returns an error explaining why the read is short."

So `Peek(n)` can return:
- `n` bytes (and nil error) if n bytes are available
- Fewer than `n` bytes (and an error) if fewer bytes are available

However, looking at the original code and how Go's `bufio.Reader.Peek` works:

When you call `Peek(i+1)`, you get back `a` which contains at most `i+1` bytes. If we have at least `i+1` bytes available, `a` will have length `i+1`. If we have fewer than `i+1` bytes available, `a` will have length equal to what's available (which could be less than `i+1`).

The **original check** `len(a) < (i+1)` ensures we have at least `i+1` bytes before accessing `a[i]`. This is a proper bounds check.

